unit-redis-1.0.0 (1) keel; urgency=low

  * The Redis component, as a fab unit (decision 0010): a plan, an overlay
    and a conf script, consumed through unit.d by every recipe that carries
    a Redis server. Redis is the third of the three engines decision 0013
    settled on, and the first one whose component is written rather than
    extracted: turnkeylinux/common has no conf/redis, no overlays/redis and
    no plans/turnkey/redis, so there was nothing in the shared tree to take
    out. The upstream appliance configured the server from its own recipe.

  * The server listens on the loopback of both families, and both are
    literal addresses: "bind ::1 127.0.0.1". Debian ships
    "bind 127.0.0.1 -::1", where the dash makes ::1 optional, so a server
    that cannot bind it starts anyway and answers one family. A name is
    never used: Debian maps ::1 to ip6-localhost and never to localhost,
    which is how the PostgreSQL appliance shipped listening on IPv4 only.

  * The declared secret is the password of an ACL user, not requirepass.
    Redis has no database account, and the vocabulary work of decision 0013
    leaves both open. This component chooses the ACL user for two reasons.
    requirepass is the default user's password, so it also locks INFO, which
    is the one thing `keel inspect` reads to report database.server.role,
    and inspect never reads a secret to get past a refusal: an appliance
    with requirepass set cannot say that it is standalone, and the role is
    the seam the replication modes of decision 0013 are built on. And
    requirepass lives in /etc/redis/redis.conf, which the package ships
    root:root 0644, so the upstream appliance wrote the Redis password into
    a world readable file and shipped turnkey-redis-pw to print it back out;
    an ACL rule takes the SHA-256 of a password instead, and this component
    writes that into a file it installs root:redis 0640.

  * So the default account is left able to run one command, INFO, with no
    key and no channel, and the administrative account is published "off",
    which is this component's version of the unusable account keel-mariadb
    publishes: a layer is published once and reused, so a password chosen at
    build time would be the same password everywhere and a random one would
    make the layer irreproducible. firstboot.d/35redispass turns the account
    on from secrets.db_password of the instance description, restarts the
    server, proves the password by connecting, and proves the other half by
    asking for a key with no credentials and requiring NOPERM.

  * Configuration is added as fragments under /etc/redis/redis.conf.d, with
    one appended line in the packaged conffile including them at the end of
    the file, where Redis's own manual says an include belongs when it is
    meant to override. The 110 kB conffile keeps every other default and
    every comment, and an upgrade has one line to merge.

  * The conf script asserts the behaviour and not the file it wrote
    (docs/traps.md): it starts the server from the real configuration, asks
    INFO on [::1] and on 127.0.0.1 in turn, and asks for a key with no
    credentials and requires the refusal. It writes no snapshot and leaves
    no directory behind.

  * No conf-vars. fab lets a unit name the build time variables its conf
    script reads, and this one reads none: the password comes from the
    instance description at first boot, not from the build.

  * No Webmin module. Debian 13 packages webmin-mysql and webmin-postgresql
    and no equivalent for Redis, so an appliance built on this component
    gets the panel core carries and no Redis page in it.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 22:00:00 +0000
